Patch 7931 (experiment, rollback netcode / GPU time): GPU_OVERLAP=1 (patch 2907) waits on per-batch fences and works with PIPE=1.

Why. test11 research placed the desktop's 66-83 ms frames inside render_frame_prepare's full wait_for_gpu (the GPU takes 50-70 ms at
3200-4000 draws). GPU_OVERLAP=1 removes that drain, but it could not be tested online: it switched PIPE off (it was on PIPE's list of
debug switches), and PIPE is a StartupMustMatch session setting, so a PC with GPU_OVERLAP=1 reported PIPE=0 and did not match its peer.
It also relied on move_to_next_frame, which only waits when a batch presents: after a mid-frame flush or an offscreen-only batch (same
frame index) the next prepare reset a command allocator, a texture-ring part and the GPU_TIME readback the GPU could still be using,
and the grave released objects two batch counts later whatever the GPU was doing.

What (all only with GPU_OVERLAP=1; off, the code paths are the old ones, plus a lock around the grave):
- Each batch signals its own value on a fence of its own right after ExecuteCommandLists. Prepare waits only for the last batch that
  used its frame index (allocator, ring part, GPU_TIME readback); after the upload halves flip (vp_parity) the FIFO thread waits for
  the last batch that read the half it is about to record into. With PIPE=1 that wait comes after the hand-off, so the submit thread
  builds batch N while the GPU finishes N-1 and the FIFO thread records N+1.
- Grave entries wait for a fence value (every executed batch, the one in the submit thread and the one recording), not a count.
- Rare in-place rewrites wait for every executed batch (font atlas, GCM_GUEST_FB, a texture slot's own upload buffer); the CPU
  scanout buffer is kept per frame index; the copy-rects target goes through the grave.
- GPU_OVERLAP is no longer on PIPE's debug list. The fence is made at backend init; if it cannot be made, GPU_OVERLAP is off.
- [OVERLAP] every 600 batches: allocator, upload-half and full waits (count, ms). The HITCH probe counts them as "gpu wait".
- The grave gets an SRW lock: tintrects_draw graves on the submit thread with PIPE=1 while the FIFO thread adds and sweeps.
Guest memory: the backend gives nothing back to the RSX command processor (its callbacks return void); the only GPU-to-guest copy is
RT_READBACK=1 (off by default), which still drains the GPU itself. So the overlap changes timing only, never what the guest reads.

